## 2. THE SHARED-SUPPLY ENGINE & LOCAL MARKET ADVANTAGE
Traditional standalone delivery platforms fail in emerging commercial hubs due to supply fragmentation and prohibitive driver idle time. Swiftly Go solves this through an automated cross-dispatch algorithm synchronized with Abia State’s daily economic rhythm:

| Time Window | Primary Demand Source | Fleet Utilized | Operational Objective |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **07:00 – 10:00** | Commuter Peak (Offices, Markets, Schools across Faulks Rd, Brass, Tower Umuahia) | Keke Napep (Passenger) | Maximum ride completion velocity, high volume, minimal driver downtime |
| **11:30 – 14:30** | Lunch Hour & Corporate Orders (Aba & Umuahia eateries) | Motorcycles (Food) + Keke (Overflow) | Fast restaurant turnaround, high average basket sizes |
| **14:30 – 17:30** | B2B Waybills & Commercial Parcels (Ariaria, Ekeoha, Cemetery to Motor Parks) | Motorcycles & Keke (Logistics) | Bulk B2B trade parcel transit before market closing |
| **17:30 – 21:00** | Evening Commuter Rush & Dinner Delivery | Keke (Passenger) + Bikes (Dinner) | Second high-margin passenger wave + evening family meals |

